   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend chapter 384 of the laws of 1998 amending the public\nauthorities law relating to the powers of Terence Cardinal Cooke health care\ncenter, in relation to extending the effectiveness thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S8822",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8822-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §5, Chap 384 of 1998",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8822\n\nSPONSOR: HANNON\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend chapter 384 of the laws of 1998 amending the public\nauthorities law relating to the powers of Terence Cardinal Cooke health\ncare center, in relation to extending the effectiveness thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo amend chapter 384 of the laws of 1998, amending the Public Authori-\nties Law, in relation to authorizing the dormitory authority to\nconstruct and finance certain facilities of Terence Cardinal Cooke\nhealth centers.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill extends the expiration date of the act from\nDecember 31, 2018, to December 31, 2023.\n\nSection two of the bill establishes the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nTerence Cardinal Cooke Health Care Center has delivered exceptional long\nand short-term care, as well as other vital health services to people\nwith diverse health and social needs since 1978. Located in the upper\nend of New York's Central Park, it is home to nurses skilled in 24-hour\ncare in a traditional nursing home setting, a premiere sub-acute reha-\nbilitation program that eases the transition from hospital to home for\npeople recovering from major illnesses or surgery, and a specialty\nhospital for children and young adults with profound developmental disa-\nbilities and other extraordinary medical needs. This legislation will\nallow the organization to be eligible for financing and construction\nservices through the Dormitory Authority of the State of New York.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nS.5371/A.7753 of 2013, SIGNED CHAP.29\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
